{"name":"Limited Lime","hex":"#eaecb9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"limited","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of limit"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["With certain (often specified) limits placed upon it.","Restricted, small, few, not plentiful."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/limited"},{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lime (material)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is an inorganic material composed primarily of calcium oxides and hydroxides. It is also the name for calcium oxide which is used as an industrial mineral and is made by heating calcium carbonate in a kiln. Calcium oxide can occur as a product of coal-seam fires and in altered limestone xenoliths in volcanic ejecta. The International Mineralogical Association recognizes lime as a mineral with the chemical formula of CaO. The word lime originates with its earliest use as building mortar and has the sense of sticking or adhering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Limestone_quarry.jpg/330px-Limestone_quarry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(material)","wikidata":"Q250423"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_lime_cycle.jpg/500px-The_lime_cycle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_lime_cycle.jpg/500px-The_lime_cycle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The lime cycle for high-calcium lime","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_lime_cycle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg/500px-Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg/1280px-Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg/500px-Lime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lime formed to piping bringing thermal water to Katlanovo spa, North Macedonia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALime_residue_thermal_water_Katlanovo_spa_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg/500px-The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb